>>
endobj
607 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 230 0 R
/E (The CAN network operates inherently asynchronously, with messages transmitted either by events or at different frequencies \(inverters at 100 Hz, VCU at 50 Hz, BMS at 10 Hz\). However, the professional MoTeC .ld telemetry format requires every channel to have a fixed synchronous sampling rate over time \(an implicit time axis derived from a single logging frequency\).)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [17]
>>
endobj
608 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 230 0 R
/T (TelemetryV2 Approach \(implemented in Rust within logs.rs\):)
/E (TelemetryV2 Approach \(implemented in Rust within logs.rs\):)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [18]
>>
endobj
609 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1679 0 R
/E (The backend intercepts CAN frames and performs synchronous resampling onto a fixed 100 Hz grid \(LD_SAMPLE_RATE_HZ\).)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [19]
>>
endobj
610 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1680 0 R
/E (The interpolation method used is Zero-Order Hold \(ZOH\). This method assumes that a physical signal remains constant at its last measured value until a new CAN message updates its state.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [20]
>>
endobj
611 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1681 0 R
/E (ZOH accurately models the behavior of discrete CAN buses without introducing artificial noise. It ensures that asynchronous signals from different subsystems remain aligned millisecond by millisecond in the output file, enabling high-integrity overlays and dynamic calculations in MoTeC i2 Pro.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [21]
>>
endobj
